1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:03,630
Hey everyone, I'm Suneet Malhotra.

2
00:00:04,520 --> 00:00:09,390
I've spent around 20 years in the
software quality and test engineering,

3
00:00:09,450 --> 00:00:14,899
working across large systems, mobile
products, cloud services, and IoT.

4
00:00:15,739 --> 00:00:19,699
And for most of that time, like
my job was to ask basic questions.

5
00:00:19,899 --> 00:00:22,529
Can we trust this software
enough to release it?

6
00:00:23,929 --> 00:00:26,609
And over the last year, that
question has changed a lot with

7
00:00:27,269 --> 00:00:29,750
respect to GenAI and AI coming in.

8
00:00:29,759 --> 00:00:33,250
Like, how do we ensure that whatever we
are helping to build and test itself?

9
00:00:34,049 --> 00:00:37,830
So I've been working on agentic
tools like Claude Code, MCP,

10
00:00:38,320 --> 00:00:39,820
and testing around them as well.

11
00:00:40,469 --> 00:00:43,229
The one thing I learned was like
demos are pretty impressive.

12
00:00:43,249 --> 00:00:50,670
You can get to from a PRD requirements
to ticket creation, to writing the

13
00:00:50,690 --> 00:00:57,250
code, testing the code, open a pull
request in minutes, as well as the

14
00:00:57,260 --> 00:01:02,759
talk then becomes like, "Hey, we
are able to get about 80% of the

15
00:01:02,979 --> 00:01:06,419
completion or percent accuracy done."

16
00:01:07,309 --> 00:01:09,729
So my question is I'm not
claiming that every agent

17
00:01:09,789 --> 00:01:12,829
produces exactly 80% correct code.

18
00:01:13,479 --> 00:01:18,809
I'm using that as a practical way to
describe a system that often works

19
00:01:18,859 --> 00:01:23,579
enough to look useful or not, but
still falls often enough that remains

20
00:01:23,579 --> 00:01:27,529
the main remaining work that really
helps matters for QA especially

21
00:01:28,529 --> 00:01:31,859
So yeah, that's where the premise, the
80 number-- percent number comes up,

22
00:01:32,439 --> 00:01:36,599
where let's start with a simple example
from a QA leader's point of view.

23
00:01:37,279 --> 00:01:40,649
An agent is asked to add a
test for a checkout flow.

24
00:01:41,489 --> 00:01:46,229
It reads the ticket, edits
the test, runs the suite, and

25
00:01:46,229 --> 00:01:47,659
reports like success, right?

26
00:01:47,889 --> 00:01:51,569
So the output may be correct, but
then what does that 80% accurate

27
00:01:51,609 --> 00:01:53,409
tell us about the release decision?

28
00:01:53,889 --> 00:01:56,909
Are we ready to the release or
do we have enough test coverage?

29
00:01:57,529 --> 00:02:02,059
Has our test returned all
the different use cases?

30
00:02:02,209 --> 00:02:03,639
Is it covered end to end?

31
00:02:04,399 --> 00:02:06,749
What are the manual checks
we have in this process?

32
00:02:06,779 --> 00:02:07,609
What's changed?

33
00:02:07,619 --> 00:02:08,889
What tools did we call?

34
00:02:09,769 --> 00:02:13,359
So these are questions like
behind the title, right?

35
00:02:13,359 --> 00:02:18,089
So I'm trying to capture the cost of the
work around the generated output, the

36
00:02:18,099 --> 00:02:23,559
checking, the evidence, the permissions,
and that's where the 80% cannot be good

37
00:02:23,559 --> 00:02:25,479
enough to create the real leverage.

38
00:02:26,049 --> 00:02:28,319
It's not good enough to
remove engineering controls.

39
00:02:28,329 --> 00:02:32,759
So that's where I think my, my, keep
coming back to these five questions.

40
00:02:33,409 --> 00:02:35,179
What exactly did the agent see?

41
00:02:35,679 --> 00:02:37,679
What context did we provide to the agent?

42
00:02:37,689 --> 00:02:38,919
What tools did it call?

43
00:02:38,959 --> 00:02:39,779
What changed?

44
00:02:40,319 --> 00:02:43,639
And if we cannot answer these five
questions, we cannot confidently say

45
00:02:43,639 --> 00:02:46,119
that this product is good to go or not.

46
00:02:47,119 --> 00:02:50,539
So from my perspective, like
what is agentic software

47
00:02:50,559 --> 00:02:51,859
development lifecycle, right?

48
00:02:52,459 --> 00:02:55,459
It comes on a stage of five stages.

49
00:02:55,469 --> 00:02:59,199
You specify like what you want in
terms of the requirements, defining the

50
00:02:59,199 --> 00:03:02,099
constraints, the acceptance criteria.

51
00:03:02,839 --> 00:03:05,589
Then this-- then you plan around it.

52
00:03:05,599 --> 00:03:09,699
You plan means that you turn the
requirements into tickets, into

53
00:03:09,699 --> 00:03:11,879
a task or sequence of actions.

54
00:03:12,879 --> 00:03:16,199
Then the build contains like
you include the ac- the code

55
00:03:16,209 --> 00:03:17,989
generation, config changes.

56
00:03:18,619 --> 00:03:21,809
You verify that run tests
are running correctly or not.

57
00:03:22,709 --> 00:03:25,989
And then by operate, what you
mean is like you monitor the

58
00:03:25,989 --> 00:03:28,349
release, roll back if necessary.

59
00:03:28,899 --> 00:03:33,979
So these are the unit of governance is
this chain, not the code artifact, right?

60
00:03:34,809 --> 00:03:38,639
And that's where the individual file
might look correct, but if the chain

61
00:03:38,649 --> 00:03:42,559
around it is broken, then we cannot
confidently approve the release.

62
00:03:42,749 --> 00:03:46,629
Maybe test can pass, but it
might-- test might hallucinate,

63
00:03:46,639 --> 00:03:47,589
it might have a wrong locator.

64
00:03:47,589 --> 00:03:52,259
A ticket can be marked as complete
while the acceptance criteria was

65
00:03:52,259 --> 00:03:54,929
never precise enough to evaluate.

66
00:03:55,689 --> 00:03:59,969
So these are the five kind of governance
of the change that I'm monitoring.

67
00:04:00,969 --> 00:04:03,219
So where does MCP fit in all this, right?

68
00:04:03,259 --> 00:04:06,179
MCP is not much, it's like
a model context protocol.

69
00:04:06,749 --> 00:04:10,119
It gives us a standard layer
between AI client and the

70
00:04:10,119 --> 00:04:11,759
tool that changes the state.

71
00:04:12,589 --> 00:04:17,769
So like in our use case, we have
MCP for Confluence, for Jira, for

72
00:04:17,809 --> 00:04:22,269
TestRail, for GitHub, and for Firebase.

73
00:04:22,459 --> 00:04:25,289
So each of these artifacts
are linked by MCPs.

74
00:04:25,849 --> 00:04:28,659
For Slack, for for Salesforce.

75
00:04:29,989 --> 00:04:34,959
So the idea is that the MCP layer defines
the tool contracts and the route requests.

76
00:04:35,039 --> 00:04:40,109
So you are able to use a Confluence
MCP to write PRD requirements based on

77
00:04:40,109 --> 00:04:43,759
your certain constraints, conditions
that you provide to the AI and the

78
00:04:43,759 --> 00:04:47,479
context, and you are able to do--
use Confluence MCP instead of writing

79
00:04:47,479 --> 00:04:51,129
manually all the requirements, you use
that to create a requirement document.

80
00:04:51,719 --> 00:04:54,759
Same thing for Jira, it helps you
create Jira tickets automatically,

81
00:04:55,239 --> 00:04:56,799
and same for-- thing for TestRail.

82
00:04:56,839 --> 00:04:58,649
You don't have to do
this process manually.

83
00:04:59,109 --> 00:05:03,449
So basically, MCP helps to helps in
reasoning and planning and really

84
00:05:03,449 --> 00:05:08,849
become theee the routing and the
system of rec-record, as well as

85
00:05:08,939 --> 00:05:11,969
making it very easy for different
tools to talk to each, each other

86
00:05:12,969 --> 00:05:15,489
And I think more important
part is the observability.

87
00:05:15,549 --> 00:05:18,909
How do we ensure like for this all
the state changing action that going

88
00:05:18,909 --> 00:05:23,019
on, how do we preserve enough context
for someone who was not in the room

89
00:05:23,019 --> 00:05:24,579
to reconstruct the decision later?

90
00:05:25,309 --> 00:05:29,909
So at a minimum, like record what the
agent received, which model, what tool

91
00:05:29,959 --> 00:05:35,249
versions we ran, what permission was
used, so that and what evidence do we

92
00:05:35,259 --> 00:05:36,779
have to support that result, right?

93
00:05:37,159 --> 00:05:41,539
So also record like in this process
like Jira approvals, what a human has

94
00:05:41,539 --> 00:05:43,179
approved in the process, rejected.

95
00:05:43,659 --> 00:05:47,729
That's where we have a clear
chain of thought, and we are not

96
00:05:47,729 --> 00:05:52,199
dependent on the agentic tools or
AI tool to give us the final state.

97
00:05:52,849 --> 00:05:55,719
So this evidence trail really
helps turn the agent into a

98
00:05:56,309 --> 00:05:58,939
something that engineering
team can inspect and work on it

99
00:05:59,939 --> 00:06:04,479
And I think the last 20% is something
that I really wanna make emphasis in on

100
00:06:04,879 --> 00:06:09,779
AI handles the-- all the structure and
the boilerplate well and is very useful

101
00:06:09,879 --> 00:06:15,689
for the repetitive transformations,
scaffolding, documentation But I think the

102
00:06:15,689 --> 00:06:20,849
last 20% is where all these edge cases,
the context, the risk, the test data

103
00:06:20,849 --> 00:06:25,589
quality, and the decisions that depend on
the consequence rather than the syntax.

104
00:06:26,089 --> 00:06:27,579
So that's where the humans come in.

105
00:06:27,589 --> 00:06:33,389
We help review the artifacts and make sure
when-- especially when the uncertainty

106
00:06:33,399 --> 00:06:36,929
is high, that's where the-- we should be
reviewing this manually, and that's where

107
00:06:36,929 --> 00:06:39,959
the 20% of quality becomes very crucial.

108
00:06:40,959 --> 00:06:46,289
And also in our case common failures are
like modes are surprisingly inconsistent.

109
00:06:46,969 --> 00:06:52,159
We might have a sparse specification,
acceptance criteria might not be clear so

110
00:06:52,159 --> 00:06:58,149
that agents might hallucinate depending
on some API or parameter name that's

111
00:06:58,149 --> 00:07:03,989
not correct, or there could be some
schema drift or a context loss, right?

112
00:07:04,239 --> 00:07:08,659
So all this can cause for a system
to break, and that's where we help

113
00:07:08,679 --> 00:07:10,359
control and measure that evidence.

114
00:07:11,359 --> 00:07:14,539
So the risk is like where humans
sit in the workflow, and they

115
00:07:14,539 --> 00:07:15,859
help decide what's the risk.

116
00:07:16,319 --> 00:07:19,449
So wherever there is a high-risk
action items that include like product

117
00:07:19,479 --> 00:07:24,499
configuration, payments, authentication,
we need to ensure that humans are in

118
00:07:24,499 --> 00:07:29,029
the loop because it has a large or
difficult to reverse consequences.

119
00:07:30,409 --> 00:07:33,949
Some medium risk actions like
regression changes can be automated

120
00:07:33,999 --> 00:07:36,739
but still need a reliable review.

121
00:07:37,919 --> 00:07:41,979
Lastly, the ro-low risk changes
can be easily documented or with--

122
00:07:42,029 --> 00:07:43,519
co-covered with the help of unit test.

123
00:07:44,519 --> 00:07:49,229
I think it's very crucial for us to
know as whether the agentic pipeline is

124
00:07:49,229 --> 00:07:52,919
improving or not, we should measure the
rework rather than the generation volume.

125
00:07:53,709 --> 00:07:57,599
So first measure is rollback frequency,
like how many times are we rolling back,

126
00:07:57,629 --> 00:08:04,509
the defect escape rate, the unsafe tool
call rate, the audit trail completeness.

127
00:08:04,579 --> 00:08:09,809
These help us to come up with the KPIs
that we can track, make sure we are

128
00:08:09,809 --> 00:08:15,249
measuring how is governance being worked
upon and put into the analytics field we

129
00:08:15,249 --> 00:08:20,019
can analyze and see can we really depend
on the agents in the workflow or not?

130
00:08:21,019 --> 00:08:23,569
So yeah, simple go/no-go
test, I would say, is-- can

131
00:08:23,569 --> 00:08:25,079
prevent a lot of wasted effort.

132
00:08:25,559 --> 00:08:30,459
So we use the pipeline to evaluate,
to create the specification, but we

133
00:08:30,459 --> 00:08:35,579
not, do not create like autonomous
end-to-end path for release approvals.

134
00:08:35,679 --> 00:08:39,409
So that's where the simple
go/no-go test is very crucial.

135
00:08:40,259 --> 00:08:46,189
We use that for a review as a time
to review whether tests are uncertain

136
00:08:46,189 --> 00:08:49,619
or not, and make sure we have the
right engineering where answer is

137
00:08:49,619 --> 00:08:51,249
not yet to be done or complete.

138
00:08:51,889 --> 00:08:55,789
So I can give you like a com--
example, like imagine like a agent

139
00:08:55,809 --> 00:08:59,359
is asked to update a checkout
workflow and add a regression test.

140
00:08:59,929 --> 00:09:00,969
Sounds simple.

141
00:09:01,139 --> 00:09:04,419
You change the payment button, update
the test, and open a pull request.

142
00:09:06,209 --> 00:09:08,169
But then it may pass the local test.

143
00:09:08,219 --> 00:09:13,069
But about which checkout
state did the agent expect?

144
00:09:13,219 --> 00:09:17,009
Was the payment provided a sand-sandbox
configuration current or not?

145
00:09:17,819 --> 00:09:19,909
So there are all these different
parameters we need to validate for that

146
00:09:20,379 --> 00:09:26,499
as well So yeah, lastly, I would s-
close it out with three ideas, right?

147
00:09:26,979 --> 00:09:30,189
First, I would say is treat the
chain as a system under test.

148
00:09:30,669 --> 00:09:33,829
The code file is only
one part of the system.

149
00:09:34,319 --> 00:09:38,569
Secondly, I would suggest is preserve the
evidence at every state-changing step.

150
00:09:39,569 --> 00:09:42,669
The goal is not we are not serving
the model, but it is a record

151
00:09:42,669 --> 00:09:45,589
that helps test, let engineers
understand the correct workflow.

152
00:09:46,659 --> 00:09:49,949
Third, I would say give autonomy
only where the risk data supports it.

153
00:09:50,889 --> 00:09:54,119
So agents should earn more autonomy
by demonstrating their reliable

154
00:09:54,129 --> 00:09:55,899
behavior under clear controls.

155
00:09:57,339 --> 00:10:00,519
So yeah, after twenty years in QA,
this is the standard I would use.

156
00:10:00,549 --> 00:10:04,749
The best agentic pipeline is not the
one that generates the most output.

157
00:10:05,409 --> 00:10:09,939
It is the one that makes uncertainty
visible, puts difficult decision in the

158
00:10:09,939 --> 00:10:14,089
front of the right people, and leaves
ev-ev-- enough evidence for the next

159
00:10:14,109 --> 00:10:15,529
engineer to understand what happened.

160
00:10:16,569 --> 00:10:16,829
Okay.

161
00:10:16,899 --> 00:10:17,499
Thank you all.

